      <floats-group id="article-31123.s16">
        <fig id="article-31123.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Vertebral Artery Vascular Territory.&#x000a0;This illustration shows the vertebral artery circulation supplying the posterior cerebral regions. The green shading highlights the cerebellar hemispheres and brainstem structures receiving blood flow from the vertebral artery. Contributed by Dr. Johannes Sobotta, (Public Domain; https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sobo_1909_3_548.png)</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="vertebral__arterya" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-31123.s17">
        <fig id="article-31123.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Anatomical Variations of Aortic Arch Arteries.&#x000a0;Variations in the branching patterns of the arteries emerging from the aortic arch occur in about 30% of individuals. This image shows normal anatomy, a bovine anomaly where common carotid arteries arise from the brachiocephalic artery, direct origins of vertebral arteries from the aortic arch (highlighted in yellow), and a rare duplication of the vertebral artery. Contributed by Ramsis Benjamin, MD, MPH.&#x000a0;</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Aortic__arch__variations" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-31123.s18">
        <fig id="article-31123.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Hemodynamic Features of the Aortic Arch and Its Branches. Three major arteries supplying the brain arise from the aortic arch: the innominate (brachiocephalic) artery proximally, followed by the left common carotid (LCC) and left subclavian (LS) arteries (4). About 15% of blood from the ascending aorta at the C7-T1 level enters the innominate artery, which bifurcates into the right subclavian (1) and right common carotid (2) arteries. Due to this branching pattern, blood flow through the right vertebral artery (0.4 cm diameter; 75-80 mL/min) is lower than through the left vertebral artery (3; 0.7-0.8 cm diameter; 100-105 mL/min). Each common carotid artery carries approximately 380&#x000a0;to 450 mL/min, influenced by body size, age, and sex. Women typically exhibit lower flow rates. Vessel diameters are indicated in centimeters. Contributed by Ramsis Benjamin, MD, MPH.</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Vertebral__Arteries" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
